Browneye Linux installer
=======================

browneye-installer is a Python 3 / ncurses installer for Browneye Linux
13.1.  It is UEFI-only and GPT-only.  It can install across several GPT
disks: on each selected disk you can delete partitions, create new ones,
and assign mount points to new or existing partitions.  Existing entries
are preserved unless you mark them for deletion.

The application and its tests use the Python standard library only.  The
live image needs the packages listed by the browneye-live meta-package.

Running
-------

  browneye-installer                 interactive ncurses installer
  browneye-installer --plain         line-oriented serial fallback
  browneye-installer --answer FILE   non-interactive install
  browneye-installer --dry-run --answer FILE
                                     plan and log, execute no mutations
  browneye-installer --plan-json --answer FILE
                                     print the validated layout and exit

The kernel command line can supply:

  browneye.answer=/path/to/answer.json
  browneye.auto=1                    same as --non-interactive
  browneye.console=serial            same as --plain
  browneye.dry_run=1                 same as --dry-run

Answer file
-----------

The file is JSON, version 2.  It describes one layout with a list of disks.
Each disk has a path and an optional list of partitions.  A partition is
either newly created (role + fstype + mountpoint + size_mib) or adopts an
existing entry (existing_number, format false).  A minimal single-disk
example is:

  {
    "version": 2,
    "layout": {
      "disks": [
        {
          "path": "/dev/vda",
          "serial": "QM00001",
          "size_bytes": 21474836480,
          "delete_partitions": [],
          "partitions": [
            {"role": "esp", "fstype": "vfat",
             "mountpoint": "/boot", "size_mib": 1024},
            {"role": "swap", "fstype": "swap", "size_mib": 2048},
            {"role": "root", "fstype": "ext4", "mountpoint": "/"}
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "package_set": "desktop",
    "hostname": "browneye",
    "admin": {"username": "admin", "password": "change-me"},
    "confirm": {"disk": "/dev/vda", "phrase": "ERASE"}
  }

``size_mib`` may be omitted to fill the remaining free region.  A partition
that is mounted at an arbitrary absolute path has role ``data``; this is
how /home is put on a second disk:

  {
    "version": 2,
    "layout": {
      "disks": [
        {
          "path": "/dev/vda",
          "partitions": [
            {"role": "esp", "fstype": "vfat",
             "mountpoint": "/boot", "size_mib": 1024},
            {"role": "swap", "fstype": "swap", "size_mib": 2048},
            {"role": "root", "fstype": "btrfs", "mountpoint": "/"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "path": "/dev/vdb",
          "partitions": [
            {"role": "data", "fstype": "ext4", "mountpoint": "/home"}
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    ...
  }

To keep an existing partition without formatting it, give its
``existing_number`` and set ``format`` to false.  The layout must contain
exactly one root (``/``) and one ESP (``/boot``); every mount point must be
unique.  Filesystems are ext4, btrfs, vfat and swap.

The answer file contains a plaintext test password.  Keep it mode 0600 and
remove it from the live media after use.  The installer never writes the
password into a log or into plan.sh.

Layout policy
-------------

  * GPT only.  MBR disks are refused with an explanation.
  * Every selected disk is edited independently.  New partitions are
    placed in free regions; the editor shows exactly which entries will be
    deleted and which will be preserved.
  * An existing EFI System Partition can be adopted as /boot, with a warning
    when it is too small for systemd-boot plus a kernel and initramfs.
    Otherwise a 1 GiB FAT32 ESP is created.
  * Root is ext4 or btrfs, at least 8 GiB when newly created, and fills the
    remaining region unless a size is given.
  * Swap is optional.
  * Data partitions can use ext4, btrfs or vfat and may be mounted at any
    absolute path except the system-owned /boot, /proc, /sys, /dev, /run
    and /tmp trees.  /home on a second disk is the common case.
  * The GPT tail gap ends at the last usable LBA, never at the device end;
    the backup GPT is never included.

Safety features
---------------

  * The live medium is detected from /run/initramfs/livedev, the
    /run/initramfs/live mount source and the BROWNEYE label, and is never
    selectable.  Failure to identify it is a hard failure.
  * Mounted, swapped, read-only, holder-bearing (LVM/RAID/dm) and
    whole-disk-filesystem devices are refused with the specific reason.
  * The complete table is snapshotted (text and binary sectors) before any
    change.  Addition/modification commands are emitted by the pure planner.
  * After writing, every untouched partition is compared by number, start,
    size, type, partition UUID, name and attributes.  A mismatch restores
    the saved table automatically before the first mkfs.
  * The confirmation screen requires the disk path and the word ERASE.
  * --dry-run executes only read-only probes and writes plan.sh; no
    mutating command and no target file write is performed.

Install flow
------------

  1. for every changed disk: snapshot, delete selected entries, write the
     full replacement GPT, re-read, verify preservation (rollback on
     mismatch)
  2. mkfs.fat, mke2fs/ext4, mkfs.btrfs or mkswap for every partition that
     needs a filesystem; blkid verifies the type and UUID
  3. mount root, the ESP at /boot, every data partition at its mount point,
     enable swap, bind /dev /proc /sys /run, efivarfs
  4. dnf --installroot: browneye-basefs in its own first transaction
  5. dnf --installroot: browneye-base + xfce-desktop + kernel/modules +
     dracut + efibootmgr + dosfstools + btrfs-progs + sudo + dnf
  6. fstab by UUID for every mount and swap area, hostname, admin user in
     wheel, root locked, first-boot marker, systemd presets and
     graphical.target
  7. depmod + dracut --force --no-hostonly
  8. chroot bootctl --esp-path=/boot --no-variables install; loader.conf and
     loader/entries/browneye-<kver>.conf by UUID; EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI
     fallback; best-effort efibootmgr NVRAM entry; /etc/kernel/cmdline for
     future kernel packages
  9. copy install.jsonl/install.log to the target and the ESP, unmount

Logs
----

  /run/browneye/install.jsonl   JSON Lines, one event/command per line
  /run/browneye/install.log     human-readable mirror
  /run/browneye/plan.json       validated layout
  /run/browneye/plan.sh         dry-run command script
  /run/browneye/backup/         sfdisk text dump + binary sector backup

On success the logs are copied to /var/log/browneye-install/ on the target
and to the root of the ESP (browneye-install.jsonl/.log).  On failure the
tail is copied to the ESP as browneye-install-failure.jsonl/.log so it
survives the reboot.  Passwords are redacted everywhere.

Tests
-----

From the repository root, without root and without touching a disk:

  python3 -m unittest discover -s package_specs/browneye-installer/tests -v

The planner tests use the JSON fixtures in tests/fixtures/.
